+## User-requested chat continuation
+
+`session-retry.ts` owns manual continuation for every shared chat runtime.
+`SessionRetry` offers Retry on the latest failed, interrupted, or length-limited
+turn only while verified idle. Busy, retrying, and unknown activity hide it,
+including when a persisted outcome still reports an older failure.
+Pending permissions and forms block continuation. If execution starts after an
+eligible click, the action still requires confirmation, a successful interrupt
+response, and a fresh active-session snapshot showing idle. Failed reads never
+count as idle.
+
+OpenCode 2.0.22 has no public run/resume endpoint. Its `prompt.resume` flag only
+wakes execution after admitting a new prompt. Retry therefore appends a short,
+explicit continuation user message to the same transcript. It does not revert,
+remove results, or resend the original request. The session's current server-owned
+model and agent stay selected. Existing tool results remain context, but the model
+may still repeat tools; this is not an exactly-once execution guarantee.
+
+The existing verified unanswered notice also exposes Retry while idle; it
+supplies availability rather than adding a new silence timer. The action always
+revalidates the server transcript and active status before continuing.
+
+The initiating user message identifies the turn. New assistant steps do not
+invalidate a stop confirmation; a newer user message does. A final transcript
+read runs after status/forms reads and again at the client's prompt-dispatch
+boundary, after asynchronous preparation. This catches locally or remotely
+admitted newer prompts observed by those reads. OpenCode has no compare-and-swap
+interrupt/prompt API: another client can still admit work between the last read
+and mutation. These checks reduce that window, not eliminate it.
+
+Clicks are serialized per runtime, directory, and session across mounted views;
+a second view clicking the same turn shares the running continuation's outcome.
+After dispatch, the same turn is suppressed locally even when the POST response
+is lost, because it may already have been admitted. Proven pre-dispatch failures
+release that suppression, including a provider circuit rejection or failed final
+turn validation. So does a rejection the engine answered: an error with a status
+that `isAmbiguousSendFailure` does not call ambiguous, so the prompt was not
+admitted. A suppressed click throws, so the control always says it did not
+continue. There is no automatic retry.
+A newer latest message is required for another continuation of that turn.
+
